From 60131dda390d2b592298fa8a7361fb2c4b0740ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 04:47:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/11] fix(#95): floor_failing_tests is read directly from the
 ConvergeResult that declares it

The defect, verbatim from the issue: "If a producer renames or stops setting one, the read silently
becomes 'clean'." Nine sites in cli.py read `getattr(result, "floor_failing_tests", ())`; the default
means "the floor holds", so a renamed field would have let converge print a verdict over a suite whose
own tests fail on current code.

Why the obvious fix is wrong: every reader receives a ConvergeResult, which declares the field and
documents `()` as "the floor holds, never unmeasured" (a run that could not measure the floor is
ungateable through its validity instead). An absent answer is therefore not a state of that object, so
the read raises rather than inventing a third value.

Converted: _completeness_verdict, _plain_terms, _final_banner_status, _format_converge (x2),
_format_converge_terse, _floor_caused, _converge_routing, _converge_action. Test doubles of
ConvergeResult gain the field.

Not closed: `failing_tests` (the engine's list, three-valued) and the other flag families.

FAULT_OPEN["floor_failing_tests"] 9 -> 0. Disarmed, the new intent test fails (1 of 1 new test that
reads the banner; the rest pass because they state the unchanged behaviour).
Affected neighbours and the four guards pass under 3.14 and 3.12; ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7.
